# Pitfalls — Smart Pointers & Ownership

## ❌ Pitfall 1: Cyclic `shared_ptr` References

```cpp
// LEAK: A and B keep each other alive forever
struct B;
struct A { std::shared_ptr<B> b; };
struct B { std::shared_ptr<A> a; };  // ← should be weak_ptr!

auto a = std::make_shared<A>();
auto b = std::make_shared<B>();
a->b = b;
b->a = a;  // cycle! neither A nor B is ever destroyed
```

**Fix**: Child-to-parent back references must be `weak_ptr`.

## ❌ Pitfall 2: `shared_ptr` as the Default Pointer

Using `shared_ptr` everywhere because it "feels safe" adds control-block allocation, reference-count updates (atomic!), and cache misses. Most ownership is *exclusive* — use `unique_ptr`.

```cpp
// ❌ Unnecessary shared ownership
std::shared_ptr<Config> cfg = std::make_shared<Config>(path);

// ✅ Single owner
std::unique_ptr<Config> cfg = std::make_unique<Config>(path);
```

## ❌ Pitfall 3: Storing `this` in a `shared_ptr`

```cpp
struct Foo : std::enable_shared_from_this<Foo> {
    std::shared_ptr<Foo> getShared() {
        return std::shared_ptr<Foo>(this);  // ❌ double-free!
        // return shared_from_this();       // ✅ correct
    }
};
```

Always derive from `enable_shared_from_this` and call `shared_from_this()` — never construct a `shared_ptr` from a raw `this`.

## ❌ Pitfall 4: Using a Moved-From `unique_ptr`

```cpp
auto p = std::make_unique<Widget>();
auto q = std::move(p);
p->doWork();  // ❌ UB: p is nullptr after move!
```

After `std::move`, the source is in a **valid but unspecified state** — treat it as `nullptr`.

## ❌ Pitfall 5: Passing `shared_ptr` by Value to Callbacks

Capturing `shared_ptr` by value in lambdas extends the lifetime unexpectedly:

```cpp
// Object lives until ALL registered callbacks are gone
buttonClickHandler = [obj = shared_ptr]() { obj->handle(); };
```

If you only need to observe, capture `weak_ptr` and `lock()` in the callback.

## Prevention Checklist

- Prefer `unique_ptr`; escalate to `shared_ptr` only when ownership is truly shared
- Back-references in cycles must be `weak_ptr`
- Use `make_unique` / `make_shared` — never naked `new`
- Pass by raw pointer/reference when not transferring ownership
- Run with ASan (`-fsanitize=address`) to catch leaks at test time

